<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Italiote League</h1><p class="st-meta">430 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.) · capital Heraclea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alliance of the Greek cities of southern Italy against the Lucanians and the tyrants of Syracuse, later led by Taras until the Roman conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Greek cities of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italiotes">Italiotes</a>, drew together in the late 5th century BCE as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a>-speaking <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lucanians">Lucanians</a> pressed on their territory and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> grew ambitious. The Achaean cities, led by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/croton">Croton</a>, formed the core, with a common sanctuary at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-hera-lacinia">Hera Lacinia</a> near Croton.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Members pledged to help any city attacked by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lucanians">Lucanians</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus</a> says the generals of a city that failed to send help were to be put to death. Its army was defeated by the Lucanians near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laus">Laus</a> in 389 BCE and, in the same year, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-elleporus">Elleporus</a> river, after which he dismantled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kaulonia">Kaulonia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hipponion">Hipponion</a>. In the mid-4th century BCE <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a> took the lead and moved the league's seat to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a>, hiring generals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under Tarentine leadership the league drew in Pyrrhus against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 280 BCE. After his departure and the surrender of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a> in 272 BCE, the Greek cities became separate allies of Rome and the league ceased to exist.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/italiote-league.jpg" alt="Italiote League" width="320" height="302"><figcaption>Image: Future Perfect at Sunrise · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magna_Graecia_ancient_colonies_and_dialects-eu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>430 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Heraclea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Italiot League</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-351&amp;focus=italiote-league" aria-label="Italiote League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4980 569 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Italiote League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5300 825L5424 988M5300 825L5304 942M5300 825L5413 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5424 988h0M5304 942h0M5413 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Heraclea. <a href="/atlas?year=-351&amp;focus=italiote-league">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

